  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nClear skies are in place across the CWA this morning and will be\nthe norm for the next few days. This will help temperatures to\ndrop this morning, in addition to the cooler airmass in place \nin the wake of yesterday's cold front. Freezing temperatures \nwill be possible in the more cold-prone locations west of the \nDivide, like Jackson, Pinedale and Hoback Junction. Dewpoints \nare around 27 degrees as of 07Z (1am), with nearly calm winds so\nwould not be surprised if temperatures drop below freezing in\nthese areas. Dewpoints are slightly higher in Star Valley,\naround 32 to 34 degrees at Afton and Alpine so temperatures look\nto stay just above freezing. \n\nThe trend this week will be the dry conditions along with breezy\nwinds, along with seasonal temperatures and mainly clear skies.\nRed Flag Warnings are in effect again today across areas east \nof the Divide, from the Cody Foothills to the Wind River Basin \nand over Natrona County. West winds, with gusts of 25 to 35 mph,\nwill be widespread across much of the CWA again this afternoon.\nEastern portions of the Bighorn Basin and most of Johnson County\nwill be the exceptions. A second cold front will reach northern\nportions around 00Z and quickly push southward through the\nevening hours. The front will be dry, as there is no available\nmoisture in place after yesterday's cold front. \n\nWednesday will be the calmest day of the week. Seasonal\ntemperatures will remain in place and the winds will not be as\nstrong as today or yesterday. West winds with gusts up to 30 mph\nwill be mainly confined to more wind prone locations, mainly\nfrom southern Lincoln County to Natrona County. Red Flag\nWarnings will be likely in these areas, as critically low RH\nvalues become widespread. Stronger winds return for Thursday,\nwith widespread gusts of 30 to 40 mph occurring for most areas.\nDaytime temperatures will also be slightly above normal for \nareas east of the Divide. Another cold front will move over the\narea Friday, causing another day of gusty winds of 30 to 40 mph\nimpacting much of the CWA. \n\nModels continue to trend toward a strong cold front impacting\nthe Cowboy State this weekend and have come more inline wrt\ntiming. Confidence is medium for widespread precipitation ahead\nof the system on Saturday. If there is any snow with this storm,\nit would be confined to the northwestern mountains and the\nnorthern end of the Bighorns Sunday night into Monday morning.",
